<!-- ─── Tech / How It's Built ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container two-col">
    <div class="reveal" data-reveal>
      <span class="eyebrow">Behind the Scenes</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Built to be expanded</h2>
      <p>
        The entire collection is stored as a single JSON file. Adding a new plant is as simple as
        adding a new entry to <code>data/plants.json</code> — no database setup, no migrations,
        no complicated tooling. The plant's detail page is generated automatically from a template.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each plant gets a unique URL like <code>/plant/lagundi</code>, which serves as the target
        for QR codes placed on physical specimens in the school garden. The QR codes can be
        printed from the <a href="/qrcodes">QR Code Index</a> page.
      </p>
      <p>
        This project is built with Express.js and EJS templating — a lightweight stack
        chosen for performance on mobile devices and ease of maintenance for school staff.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="reveal" data-reveal>
      <div class="tech-card">
        <h3>Project Stack</h3>
        <ul class="tech-list">
          <li><strong>Backend:</strong> Express.js (Node.js)</li>
          <li><strong>Templating:</strong> EJS — server-side rendered for instant QR loads</li>
          <li><strong>Database:</strong> JSON file — one entry per plant</li>
          <li><strong>Search:</strong> Fuse.js — client-side fuzzy matching</li>
          <li><strong>QR Codes:</strong> QRCode.js — generated per plant URL</li>
          <li><strong>Styling:</strong> Custom CSS — mobile-first, no framework</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

